{
  "teamId": 144,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 Atlanta Braves were supposed to be a team still in transition, a franchise rebuilding on the fly under first-year manager Brian Snitker with a roster full of unproven youth and quiet veterans. Instead, they announced themselves immediately. On Opening Day, Nick Markakis launched a three-run homer to right center, a statement that this group intended to compete. The Braves played with an energy that surprised the baseball world, carrying a winning record through April and into a scorching May that featured Dansby Swanson walk-off heroics, Ozzie Albies' grand slam, and the unforgettable Charlie Culberson two-run shot that felt like a lightning bolt from nowhere. By the end of May the Braves were one of the best stories in baseball.\n\nJune continued Atlanta's surprising run, even as the bullpen began to show cracks. Manny Machado and the Nationals took turns punishing Atlanta's relievers in high-leverage spots, and the summer began to wear on a young roster. July brought the only losing month of the season, a 10-13 stretch where the team's inexperience showed and opponents found ways to deliver knockout blows in the late innings. But the Braves refused to fold. August brought a resurgence — a 17-13 month powered by Ronald Acuña Jr.'s breathtaking tools and timely contributions from Markakis and Inciarte — even as the bullpen continued to be the team's Achilles heel in individual games.\n\nSeptember confirmed what had been building all year: Atlanta would win the NL East. Ender Inciarte's three-run homer in early September captured the spirit of a team that kept finding ways to score. The Braves finished 90-72, their first division title since 2013, built on a balanced attack that scored 759 runs and a rotation anchored by Mike Foltynewicz that kept the ERA at a tidy 3.75. It was a season that announced the arrival of a new core.\n\nThe postseason brought the Los Angeles Dodgers in the NLDS, and for a moment Atlanta dared to dream. Acuña's grand slam in Game Four and Freeman's solo shot showed the Braves still had firepower, and Arodys Vizcaíno punched out Max Muncy in a crucial moment that briefly steadied the ship. But the Dodgers were battle-tested where Atlanta was green. Brad Brach's David Freese single and Manny Machado's three-run homer off Chad Sobotka in Game Five extinguished the Braves' hopes. The October exit stung, but it also felt like a promise — this young team would be back.",
